  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1119 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nTemperatures continue to modify into the start of the weekend as \ndaytime highs Saturday climb above average with many locations \nreaching the low to mid 70s under sunny skies. Saturday night \nremains mild in the advance of the next cold front, with the \novernight lows in the 50s. While the latest guidance is not \nindicating much of an uptick in the low clouds, some fog may try \nto develop with the milder air. \n\nA storm system across the Central Plains with a surface low that \nrides along the Red River sends the next cold front across the \nregion on Sunday morning. The front is forecast to remain dry \nthrough our region with any light precipitation across the state \noccurring farther east/southeast. Breezy north to northwesterly \nwinds establish behind the front with gusts in the 30 to 35 mph \nrange. The flow advects a drier and cooler airmass through the \nregion as we start next week. Overnight lows Sunday night into \nMonday morning return to the 30s to low 40s while the afternoon \nhighs on Monday hold in the low to mid 60s. The temperatures are \nthen forecast to modify a bit more quickly heading into midweek \nwhere the afternoon highs return to the 70s for many while the \nmorning lows gradually climb as well.\n\nThe medium range models then hint that the next storm system may \napproach late Wednesday through Thursday, which would be finally \nwhen we see rain chances return into our forecast, focused in \neastern areas for now.",
